No live Rhylthyme tools are available in this offline session, so I cannot call `validate_program`, `analyze_schedule`, `visualize_schedule`, or return a live URL/Gantt. Below is the hand-built program JSON plus the expected schedule I derived by applying the authoring rules.

Improved question:  
Instead of only asking “which activities depend on which, and which can run at the same time?”, ask: **“At every handoff and rest boundary, which partner must be finished, which resource must be free, and which per-round barrier must be cleared before the next activity can start?”**

Answer:  
The session implies several simultaneous barriers that the plain text states but does not give explicit timing to: both partners must finish warm-up before the first superset starts; Partner A’s row cannot start until Partner B has set the dumbbells down; Partner B’s bench cannot start until Partner A racks the bar; each Superset A rest must wait for both swap exercises of that round; Superset B does not start until the final Superset A rest ends; Superset B swaps only after both the initial overhead press and initial pull-ups are complete; the final cool-down waits for all Superset B work to finish. These are encoded with `afterStep`, `compound`, `instances: "each"`, `instances: "all"`, and `instances: "any"`. No extra timed inferred step is needed; the revealed dependencies are instantaneous handoff/barrier relationships already present in the step durations.

Expected schedule:  
`0:00` rowing, `5:00` arm circles/band pull-aparts, `8:00` Superset A round 1 starts, `10:50` round 2 starts, `13:40` Superset B round 1 starts, `17:10` round 2 starts, `18:40` cool-down starts, `23:40` finish early as the work allows.

